3. Community participation in the implementation of protection for Victims of Gender-Based Violence and Children may be carried out through: a. providing protection for victims of gender-based violence and children;
b. providing emergency assistance;
c. providing advocacy to victims and/or the community regarding the handling of cases of gender-based violence and children;
d. assisting in the process of submitting applications for protection orders;
e. conveying information to the competent authorities related to cases of gender-based violence and children; f. fostering local wisdom with the principles of non-discrimination and the best interests of victims in handling gender-based violence and children;
g. organizing strengthening of community groups in handling gender-based violence and children; h. disseminating information about laws and regulations related to gender-based violence and children.

Article 3

1. The objectives of implementing protection for victims of gender-based violence and children are:a. to prevent all forms of gender-based violence and violence against children, occurring within the scope of the household and/or community;
b. to provide protection;
c. to provide legal accompaniment/assistance;
d. to seek recovery and social reintegration; and/or e. to increase community participation.
2. The objectives of implementing protection for victims of gender-based violence and children as referred to in paragraph (1), include aspects of:a. prevention;
b. service and assistance;
c. reunification; andd. empowerment.

Article 21

1. The Regional Government is obligated to empower victims of violence.
2. Empowerment of victims of violence as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be implemented by the Women, Children and Youth Protection Institution.
3. In empowering victims of violence as referred to in paragraph (1), the Regional Government shall prepare empowerment programs in the economic field, which include: a. providing the necessary requirements for entrepreneurship training for victims of violence, in order to increase knowledge, attitudes, and business skills;
b. facilitating the implementation of various job training and skills training;
c. providing assistance in developing productive economic enterprises;
d. exploring cooperation with small, medium and large enterprises, as well as financial institutions to develop businesses of victims of violence;
e. facilitating the provision of capital for victims of violence; and f. expanding access to information and promoting the products of victims of violence.
4. In empowering victims of violence as referred to in paragraph (1), the Regional Government may establish cooperation with job training institutions organized by the private sector.

Article 7

1. The obligations and responsibilities of the Regional Government as referred to in Article 6 letter a, include:
a. implementing protection policies against gender-based violence and children established by the government;
b. establishing policies, programs, and activities for protection against gender-based violence and children;
c. conducting cooperation in the implementation of protection for women and children from violence;
d. providing support for facilities and infrastructure for the implementation of protection against gender-based violence and children;
e. allocating budget for the implementation of protection against gender-based violence and children in accordance with regional financial capacity; and
f. fostering and supervising the implementation of protection against gender-based violence and children.
2. In order to carry out the obligations and responsibilities as referred to in paragraph (1), the regional government establishes action programs and activities for the protection of gender and children in 1 (one) Regional Action Plan as a basis for SKPD in implementing protection against gender-based violence and children.
3. Further provisions regarding the Regional Action Plan as referred to in paragraph (2), shall be regulated by a Mayor's Regulation.

CHAPTER III

RIGHTS OF VICTIMS

Article 4

Every victim of gender-based violence and child victim of violence has the right to receive:

a. protection for the security of self, family, and property, and freedom from threats related to testimony that will be, is being, or has been given;
b. participation in the process of selecting and determining the form of protection and security support;
c. information regarding case developments and court decisions; d. services that are fast, appropriate, comfortable, and in accordance with needs; e. recovery and social reintegration;
f. legal assistance, psychological, spiritual guidance, economic, social and interpreter.

Article 24

1. In order to achieve the objectives of implementing protection against gender-based violence and children as referred to in Article 3, the Regional Government shall cooperate with: a. the Government;
b. the Provincial Government;
c. other regency/city governments; and d. the community.
2. Cooperation as referred to in paragraph (1) includes: a. exchange of data and information;
b. rehabilitation of victims of violence;
c. return and social reintegration; and d. provision of evidence and witnesses, and followed up in accordance with applicable law.
3. Cooperation as referred to in paragraph (1) and paragraph (2) shall be implemented in accordance with laws and regulations.

CHAPTER VIII

GUIDANCE AND SUPERVISION

Article 26

1. The Regional Government is obligated to conduct guidance and supervision of the implementation of protection against gender-based violence and children.
2. Guidance as referred to in paragraph (1) includes: a. minimum service standards (SPM);
b. technical guidance and training;
c. provision of facilities;
d. monitoring; and e. evaluation.
3. Supervision as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be conducted with the following principles: a. professional;
b. transparent; and c. accountable.
4. Further provisions regarding the procedures for guidance and supervision by the Regional Government as referred to in paragraph (2) and paragraph (3) shall be regulated by Mayor Regulation.

Article 14

1. The Regional Government conducts empowerment and awareness to families, parents, and the community by providing information, guidance and/or counseling to prevent the occurrence of criminal acts of gender-based violence and children.
2. In addition to empowerment and awareness as referred to in paragraph (1), the Regional Government makes efforts as follows:
a. increasing the number and quality of education both formal and non-formal and informal;
b. opening accessibility to obtain education, training, funding, income enhancement and social services;
c. opening employment opportunities;
d. building community participation and concern to implement prevention and protection against gender-based violence and children;
e. building and providing a complete and easily accessible information system;
f. building cooperative networks with law enforcement officers, government apparatus, universities and various non-governmental organizations that work and/or care about gender and children; and
g. opening complaint posts for protection against gender-based violence and children.

Article 20

1. Forms of Reunification provided to victims of gender-based violence and children are as follows: a. repatriation/returning home;
b. social reintegration.
2. Return services as referred to in paragraph (1) letter a include: a. coordinating with relevant agencies;
b. implementing the victim return process; and c. providing protection to the victim's destination.
3. Social reintegration services as referred to in paragraph (1) letter b include: a. assistance in the process of reunification with the victim's family;
b. providing information to the local community for the victim's reunification with their environment;
c. providing assurance that the family and community are ready to accept the victim back; and d. implementing economic empowerment.

Article 16

Forms of services and assistance for victims of gender-based violence and children organized by the Women, Children and Youth Protection Institution, include:
a. medical;
b. medicolegal;
c. psychosocial;
d. legal;
e. economic independence

Article 17

The provision of services and assistance to victims of gender-based violence and children shall be carried out with the following principles: a. fast, safe, and empathetic;
b. guarantee of confidentiality;
c. easily accessible; and
d. free of charge.

Article 2

Protection of victims of gender-based violence and children is based on Pancasila and grounded in the 1945 Constitution, as well as basic principles that include:

a. non-discrimination;
b. best interests of the victim;
c. justice and gender equality;
d. protection of victims;
e. survival, and growth and development of children; f. respect for the opinion of children;
g. transparency;
h. integration;
i. empowering; andj. confidentiality of victims.

